1
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:09,599
I now fell into a regular routine of apprenticeship life, which was varied

2
00:00:09,599 --> 00:00:14,240
beyond the limits of the village and the marshes, by no more remarkable circumstance

3
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:20,159
than the arrival of my birthday and my paying another visit to Miss Havisham. I found Miss

4
00:00:20,159 --> 00:00:25,560
Sarah Pocket still on duty at the gate; I found Miss Havisham just as I had left her,

5
00:00:25,561 --> 00:00:31,599
and she spoke of Estella in the very same way, if not in the very same words. The

6
00:00:31,599 --> 00:00:36,278
interview lasted but a few minutes, and she gave me a guinea when I was going,

7
00:00:36,278 --> 00:00:42,718
and told me to come again on my next birthday. I may mention at once that this became an annual

8
00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:48,400
custom. I tried to decline taking the guinea on the first occasion, but with no better effect

9
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:56,240
than causing her to ask me very angrily, if I expected more? Then, and after that, I took it.

10
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:01,479
So unchanging was the dull old house, the yellow light in the darkened room,

11
00:01:01,479 --> 00:01:05,240
the faded spectre in the chair by the dressing-table glass,

12
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:11,000
that I felt as if the stopping of the clocks had stopped Time in that mysterious place, and,

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,438
while I and everything else outside it grew older, it stood still. Daylight never entered the house

14
00:01:18,438 --> 00:01:24,958
as to my thoughts and remembrances of it, any more than as to the actual fact. It bewildered me,

15
00:01:24,958 --> 00:01:31,159
and under its influence I continued at heart to hate my trade and to be ashamed of home.

16
00:01:31,159 --> 00:01:37,240
Imperceptibly I became conscious of a change in Biddy, however. Her shoes came up at the heel,

17
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:44,039
her hair grew bright and neat, her hands were always clean. She was not beautiful,—she was

18
00:01:44,039 --> 00:01:50,080
common, and could not be like Estella,—but she was pleasant and wholesome and sweet-tempered.

19
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:54,000
She had not been with us more than a year (I remember her being newly out

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,080
of mourning at the time it struck me), when I observed to myself one evening that she had

21
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:04,438
curiously thoughtful and attentive eyes; eyes that were very pretty and very good.

22
00:02:04,438 --> 00:02:10,400
It came of my lifting up my own eyes from a task I was poring at—writing some passages from a book,

23
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:16,000
to improve myself in two ways at once by a sort of stratagem—and seeing Biddy observant

24
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,919
of what I was about. I laid down my pen, and Biddy stopped in her needlework without laying it down.

25
00:02:23,919 --> 00:02:31,319
“Biddy,” said I, “how do you manage it? Either I am very stupid, or you are very clever.”

26
00:02:31,319 --> 00:02:36,159
“What is it that I manage? I don’t know,” returned Biddy, smiling.

27
00:02:36,159 --> 00:02:41,519
She managed our whole domestic life, and wonderfully too; but I did not mean that,

28
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:45,039
though that made what I did mean more surprising.

29
00:02:45,039 --> 00:02:50,638
“How do you manage, Biddy,” said I, “to learn everything that I learn, and always to keep up

30
00:02:50,638 --> 00:02:56,638
with me?” I was beginning to be rather vain of my knowledge, for I spent my birthday guineas on it,

31
00:02:56,638 --> 00:03:00,958
and set aside the greater part of my pocket-money for similar investment;

32
00:03:00,958 --> 00:03:06,199
though I have no doubt, now, that the little I knew was extremely dear at the price.

33
00:03:06,199 --> 00:03:10,560
“I might as well ask you,” said Biddy, “how you manage?”

34
00:03:10,561 --> 00:03:13,840
“No; because when I come in from the forge of a night,

35
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:19,759
any one can see me turning to at it. But you never turn to at it, Biddy.”

36
00:03:19,759 --> 00:03:25,680
“I suppose I must catch it like a cough,” said Biddy, quietly; and went on with her sewing.

37
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:30,319
Pursuing my idea as I leaned back in my wooden chair, and looked at Biddy sewing

38
00:03:30,319 --> 00:03:36,438
away with her head on one side, I began to think her rather an extraordinary girl. For

39
00:03:36,438 --> 00:03:41,797
I called to mind now, that she was equally accomplished in the terms of our trade,

40
00:03:41,798 --> 00:03:47,919
and the names of our different sorts of work, and our various tools. In short,

41
00:03:47,919 --> 00:03:57,319
whatever I knew, Biddy knew. Theoretically, she was already as good a blacksmith as I, or better.

42
00:03:57,319 --> 00:04:02,240
“You are one of those, Biddy,” said I, “who make the most of every chance. You

43
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:06,759
never had a chance before you came here, and see how improved you are!”

44
00:04:06,759 --> 00:04:11,400
Biddy looked at me for an instant, and went on with her sewing. “I was your

45
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:15,718
first teacher though; wasn’t I?” said she, as she sewed.

46
00:04:15,719 --> 00:04:20,278
“Biddy!” I exclaimed, in amazement. “Why, you are crying!”

47
00:04:20,278 --> 00:04:26,359
“No I am not,” said Biddy, looking up and laughing. “What put that in your head?”

48
00:04:26,359 --> 00:04:31,838
What could have put it in my head but the glistening of a tear as it dropped on her work?

49
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:37,520
I sat silent, recalling what a drudge she had been until Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt successfully

50
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:44,759
overcame that bad habit of living, so highly desirable to be got rid of by some people. I

51
00:04:44,759 --> 00:04:49,840
recalled the hopeless circumstances by which she had been surrounded in the miserable little shop

52
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:54,400
and the miserable little noisy evening school, with that miserable old bundle of incompetence

53
00:04:54,400 --> 00:05:00,479
always to be dragged and shouldered. I reflected that even in those untoward times there must have

54
00:05:00,479 --> 00:05:05,838
been latent in Biddy what was now developing, for, in my first uneasiness and discontent I

55
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:13,840
had turned to her for help, as a matter of course. Biddy sat quietly sewing, shedding no more tears,

56
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:18,599
and while I looked at her and thought about it all, it occurred to me that perhaps I had

57
00:05:18,599 --> 00:05:24,359
not been sufficiently grateful to Biddy. I might have been too reserved, and should have patronised

58
00:05:24,359 --> 00:05:30,919
her more (though I did not use that precise word in my meditations) with my confidence.

59
00:05:30,919 --> 00:05:36,680
“Yes, Biddy,” I observed, when I had done turning it over, “you were my first teacher,

60
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:42,319
and that at a time when we little thought of ever being together like this, in this kitchen.”

61
00:05:42,319 --> 00:05:47,958
“Ah, poor thing!” replied Biddy. It was like her self-forgetfulness to transfer the remark

62
00:05:47,958 --> 00:05:55,118
to my sister, and to get up and be busy about her, making her more comfortable; “that’s sadly true!”

63
00:05:55,118 --> 00:05:58,400
“Well!” said I, “we must talk together a little more,

64
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:01,560
as we used to do. And I must consult you a little more,

65
00:06:01,561 --> 00:06:08,638
as I used to do. Let us have a quiet walk on the marshes next Sunday, Biddy, and a long chat.”

66
00:06:08,638 --> 00:06:13,638
My sister was never left alone now; but Joe more than readily undertook the care of her

67
00:06:13,638 --> 00:06:22,039
on that Sunday afternoon, and Biddy and I went out together. It was summer-time, and lovely weather.

68
00:06:22,039 --> 00:06:26,519
When we had passed the village and the church and the churchyard, and were out on the marshes and

69
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:32,561
began to see the sails of the ships as they sailed on, I began to combine Miss Havisham and Estella

70
00:06:32,561 --> 00:06:39,000
with the prospect, in my usual way. When we came to the river-side and sat down on the bank, with

71
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,638
the water rippling at our feet, making it all more quiet than it would have been without that sound,

72
00:06:44,638 --> 00:06:50,798
I resolved that it was a good time and place for the admission of Biddy into my inner confidence.

73
00:06:50,798 --> 00:06:56,479
“Biddy,” said I, after binding her to secrecy, “I want to be a gentleman.”

74
00:06:56,479 --> 00:07:02,479
“O, I wouldn’t, if I was you!” she returned. “I don’t think it would answer.”

75
00:07:02,479 --> 00:07:09,319
“Biddy,” said I, with some severity, “I have particular reasons for wanting to be a gentleman.”

76
00:07:09,319 --> 00:07:13,598
“You know best, Pip; but don’t you think you are happier as you are?”

77
00:07:13,599 --> 00:07:19,561
“Biddy,” I exclaimed, impatiently, “I am not at all happy as I am. I am disgusted with my

78
00:07:19,561 --> 00:07:25,840
calling and with my life. I have never taken to either, since I was bound. Don’t be absurd.”

79
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:32,159
“Was I absurd?” said Biddy, quietly raising her eyebrows; “I am sorry for that;

80
00:07:32,159 --> 00:07:36,840
I didn’t mean to be. I only want you to do well, and to be comfortable.”

81
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:43,479
“Well, then, understand once for all that I never shall or can be comfortable—or anything but

82
00:07:43,479 --> 00:07:50,479
miserable—there, Biddy!—unless I can lead a very different sort of life from the life I lead now.”

83
00:07:50,479 --> 00:07:54,919
“That’s a pity!” said Biddy, shaking her head with a sorrowful air.

84
00:07:54,919 --> 00:07:59,359
Now, I too had so often thought it a pity, that, in the singular kind of quarrel with

85
00:07:59,359 --> 00:08:04,958
myself which I was always carrying on, I was half inclined to shed tears of vexation

86
00:08:04,958 --> 00:08:12,118
and distress when Biddy gave utterance to her sentiment and my own. I told her she was right,

87
00:08:12,118 --> 00:08:17,519
and I knew it was much to be regretted, but still it was not to be helped.

88
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:23,199
“If I could have settled down,” I said to Biddy, plucking up the short grass within reach, much as

89
00:08:23,199 --> 00:08:28,958
I had once upon a time pulled my feelings out of my hair and kicked them into the brewery wall,—“if

90
00:08:28,958 --> 00:08:33,520
I could have settled down and been but half as fond of the forge as I was when I was little,

91
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:38,520
I know it would have been much better for me. You and I and Joe would have wanted nothing then,

92
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:42,840
and Joe and I would perhaps have gone partners when I was out of my time, and I might even have

93
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:47,599
grown up to keep company with you, and we might have sat on this very bank on a fine Sunday,

94
00:08:47,599 --> 00:08:52,879
quite different people. I should have been good enough for you; shouldn’t I, Biddy?”

95
00:08:52,879 --> 00:08:57,559
Biddy sighed as she looked at the ships sailing on, and returned for answer,

96
00:08:57,559 --> 00:09:04,240
“Yes; I am not over-particular.” It scarcely sounded flattering, but I knew she meant well.

97
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:09,039
“Instead of that,” said I, plucking up more grass and chewing a blade or two,

98
00:09:09,039 --> 00:09:16,118
“see how I am going on. Dissatisfied, and uncomfortable, and—what would it signify to me,

99
00:09:16,118 --> 00:09:19,840
being coarse and common, if nobody had told me so!”

100
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:22,319
Biddy turned her face suddenly towards mine,

101
00:09:22,319 --> 00:09:27,240
and looked far more attentively at me than she had looked at the sailing ships.

102
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:32,080
“It was neither a very true nor a very polite thing to say,” she remarked,

103
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:36,278
directing her eyes to the ships again. “Who said it?”

104
00:09:36,278 --> 00:09:41,919
I was disconcerted, for I had broken away without quite seeing where I was going to. It was not to

105
00:09:41,919 --> 00:09:48,080
be shuffled off now, however, and I answered, “The beautiful young lady at Miss Havisham’s,

106
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:52,879
and she’s more beautiful than anybody ever was, and I admire her dreadfully,

107
00:09:52,879 --> 00:09:59,000
and I want to be a gentleman on her account.” Having made this lunatic confession, I began

108
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,638
to throw my torn-up grass into the river, as if I had some thoughts of following it.

109
00:10:04,638 --> 00:10:05,840
“Do you want to be a gentleman,

110
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:11,879
to spite her or to gain her over?” Biddy quietly asked me, after a pause.

111
00:10:11,879 --> 00:10:15,000
“I don’t know,” I moodily answered.

112
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:21,118
“Because, if it is to spite her,” Biddy pursued, “I should think—but you know best—that might be

113
00:10:21,118 --> 00:10:26,039
better and more independently done by caring nothing for her words. And if it is to gain

114
00:10:26,039 --> 00:10:33,000
her over, I should think—but you know best—she was not worth gaining over.”

115
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:38,400
Exactly what I myself had thought, many times. Exactly what was perfectly manifest to me at the

116
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:45,240
moment. But how could I, a poor dazed village lad, avoid that wonderful inconsistency into

117
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:53,159
which the best and wisest of men fall every day? “It may be all quite true,” said I to Biddy,

118
00:10:53,159 --> 00:10:58,680
“but I admire her dreadfully.” In short, I turned over on my face

119
00:10:58,680 --> 00:11:04,000
when I came to that, and got a good grasp on the hair on each side of my head, and wrenched

120
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:09,759
it well. All the while knowing the madness of my heart to be so very mad and misplaced,

121
00:11:09,759 --> 00:11:14,919
that I was quite conscious it would have served my face right, if I had lifted it up by my hair,

122
00:11:14,919 --> 00:11:20,479
and knocked it against the pebbles as a punishment for belonging to such an idiot.

123
00:11:20,479 --> 00:11:26,919
Biddy was the wisest of girls, and she tried to reason no more with me. She put her hand,

124
00:11:26,919 --> 00:11:33,039
which was a comfortable hand though roughened by work, upon my hands, one after another, and

125
00:11:33,039 --> 00:11:39,759
gently took them out of my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder in a soothing way, while with

126
00:11:39,759 --> 00:11:45,840
my face upon my sleeve I cried a little,—exactly as I had done in the brewery yard,—and felt

127
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:54,159
vaguely convinced that I was very much ill-used by somebody, or by everybody; I can’t say which.

128
00:11:54,159 --> 00:11:58,919
“I am glad of one thing,” said Biddy, “and that is, that you have felt you

129
00:11:58,919 --> 00:12:03,319
could give me your confidence, Pip. And I am glad of another thing, and that is,

130
00:12:03,319 --> 00:12:08,519
that of course you know you may depend upon my keeping it and always so far deserving it.

131
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:13,638
If your first teacher (dear! such a poor one, and so much in need of being taught

132
00:12:13,638 --> 00:12:18,080
herself!) had been your teacher at the present time, she thinks she knows what

133
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:24,638
lesson she would set. But it would be a hard one to learn, and you have got beyond her,

134
00:12:24,638 --> 00:12:33,638
and it’s of no use now.” So, with a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank, and said,

135
00:12:33,638 --> 00:12:38,958
with a fresh and pleasant change of voice, “Shall we walk a little farther, or go home?”

136
00:12:38,958 --> 00:12:42,319
“Biddy,” I cried, getting up, putting my arm round her neck,

137
00:12:42,319 --> 00:12:45,958
and giving her a kiss, “I shall always tell you everything.”

138
00:12:45,958 --> 00:12:48,080
“Till you’re a gentleman,” said Biddy.

139
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:52,719
“You know I never shall be, so that’s always. Not that I have any occasion to tell you anything,

140
00:12:52,719 --> 00:12:57,118
for you know everything I know,—as I told you at home the other night.”

141
00:12:57,118 --> 00:13:03,359
“Ah!” said Biddy, quite in a whisper, as she looked away at the ships. And then repeated,

142
00:13:03,359 --> 00:13:10,118
with her former pleasant change, “shall we walk a little farther, or go home?”

143
00:13:10,118 --> 00:13:16,039
I said to Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did so, and the summer afternoon toned

144
00:13:16,039 --> 00:13:21,638
down into the summer evening, and it was very beautiful. I began to consider whether I was

145
00:13:21,638 --> 00:13:27,359
not more naturally and wholesomely situated, after all, in these circumstances, than playing beggar

146
00:13:27,359 --> 00:13:35,118
my neighbour by candle-light in the room with the stopped clocks, and being despised by Estella. I

147
00:13:35,118 --> 00:13:39,240
thought it would be very good for me if I could get her out of my head, with all the rest of those

148
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:45,118
remembrances and fancies, and could go to work determined to relish what I had to do, and stick

149
00:13:45,118 --> 00:13:51,438
to it, and make the best of it. I asked myself the question whether I did not surely know that if

150
00:13:51,438 --> 00:13:57,679
Estella were beside me at that moment instead of Biddy, she would make me miserable? I was obliged

151
00:13:57,680 --> 00:14:05,879
to admit that I did know it for a certainty, and I said to myself, “Pip, what a fool you are!”

152
00:14:05,879 --> 00:14:11,400
We talked a good deal as we walked, and all that Biddy said seemed right. Biddy was never

153
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:17,039
insulting, or capricious, or Biddy to-day and somebody else to-morrow; she would have derived

154
00:14:17,039 --> 00:14:22,080
only pain, and no pleasure, from giving me pain; she would far rather have wounded her

155
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:28,638
own breast than mine. How could it be, then, that I did not like her much the better of the two?

156
00:14:28,638 --> 00:14:35,118
“Biddy,” said I, when we were walking homeward, “I wish you could put me right.”

157
00:14:35,118 --> 00:14:37,958
“I wish I could!” said Biddy.

158
00:14:37,958 --> 00:14:42,000
“If I could only get myself to fall in love with you,—you don’t mind my

159
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,559
speaking so openly to such an old acquaintance?”

160
00:14:45,559 --> 00:14:49,958
“Oh dear, not at all!” said Biddy. “Don’t mind me.”

161
00:14:49,958 --> 00:14:53,879
“If I could only get myself to do it, that would be the thing for me.”

162
00:14:53,879 --> 00:14:57,559
“But you never will, you see,” said Biddy.

163
00:14:57,559 --> 00:15:01,599
It did not appear quite so unlikely to me that evening, as it would have done

164
00:15:01,599 --> 00:15:06,559
if we had discussed it a few hours before. I therefore observed I was not quite sure

165
00:15:06,559 --> 00:15:13,319
of that. But Biddy said she was, and she said it decisively. In my heart I believed her to

166
00:15:13,319 --> 00:15:20,479
be right; and yet I took it rather ill, too, that she should be so positive on the point.

167
00:15:20,479 --> 00:15:24,000
When we came near the churchyard, we had to cross an embankment,

168
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:30,479
and get over a stile near a sluice-gate. There started up, from the gate, or from the rushes,

169
00:15:30,479 --> 00:15:36,598
or from the ooze (which was quite in his stagnant way), Old Orlick.

170
00:15:36,599 --> 00:15:40,559
“Halloa!” he growled, “where are you two going?”

171
00:15:40,559 --> 00:15:43,559
“Where should we be going, but home?”

172
00:15:43,559 --> 00:15:48,278
“Well, then,” said he, “I’m jiggered if I don’t see you home!”

173
00:15:48,278 --> 00:15:52,479
This penalty of being jiggered was a favourite supposititious case of his.

174
00:15:52,479 --> 00:15:58,359
He attached no definite meaning to the word that I am aware of, but used it, like his own

175
00:15:58,359 --> 00:16:06,679
pretended Christian name, to affront mankind, and convey an idea of something savagely damaging.

176
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:10,958
When I was younger, I had had a general belief that if he had jiggered me personally,

177
00:16:10,958 --> 00:16:14,798
he would have done it with a sharp and twisted hook.

178
00:16:14,798 --> 00:16:20,240
Biddy was much against his going with us, and said to me in a whisper, “Don’t let him come;

179
00:16:20,240 --> 00:16:26,879
I don’t like him.” As I did not like him either, I took the liberty of saying that we thanked him,

180
00:16:26,879 --> 00:16:32,680
but we didn’t want seeing home. He received that piece of information with a yell of laughter,

181
00:16:32,680 --> 00:16:36,241
and dropped back, but came slouching after us at a little distance.

182
00:16:37,118 --> 00:16:41,199
Curious to know whether Biddy suspected him of having had a hand in that murderous attack of

183
00:16:41,199 --> 00:16:46,798
which my sister had never been able to give any account, I asked her why she did not like him.

184
00:16:46,798 --> 00:16:52,199
“Oh!” she replied, glancing over her shoulder as he slouched after us,

185
00:16:52,199 --> 00:16:56,798
“because I—I am afraid he likes me.”

186
00:16:56,798 --> 00:17:00,719
“Did he ever tell you he liked you?” I asked indignantly.

187
00:17:00,719 --> 00:17:06,719
“No,” said Biddy, glancing over her shoulder again, “he never told me so;

188
00:17:06,719 --> 00:17:11,638
but he dances at me, whenever he can catch my eye.”

189
00:17:11,638 --> 00:17:15,240
However novel and peculiar this testimony of attachment,

190
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:19,759
I did not doubt the accuracy of the interpretation. I was very hot indeed

191
00:17:19,759 --> 00:17:25,599
upon Old Orlick’s daring to admire her; as hot as if it were an outrage on myself.

192
00:17:25,599 --> 00:17:29,840
“But it makes no difference to you, you know,” said Biddy, calmly.

193
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:34,879
“No, Biddy, it makes no difference to me; only I don’t like it; I don’t approve of it.”

194
00:17:34,879 --> 00:17:39,479
“Nor I neither,” said Biddy. “Though that makes no difference to you.”

195
00:17:39,479 --> 00:17:43,400
“Exactly,” said I; “but I must tell you I should have no opinion of you,

196
00:17:43,400 --> 00:17:47,118
Biddy, if he danced at you with your own consent.”

197
00:17:47,118 --> 00:17:49,479
I kept an eye on Orlick after that night,

198
00:17:49,479 --> 00:17:53,519
and, whenever circumstances were favourable to his dancing at Biddy,

199
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:59,759
got before him to obscure that demonstration. He had struck root in Joe’s establishment,

200
00:17:59,759 --> 00:18:05,359
by reason of my sister’s sudden fancy for him, or I should have tried to get him dismissed.

201
00:18:05,359 --> 00:18:12,039
He quite understood and reciprocated my good intentions, as I had reason to know thereafter.

202
00:18:12,039 --> 00:18:18,159
And now, because my mind was not confused enough before, I complicated its confusion fifty

203
00:18:18,159 --> 00:18:23,000
thousand-fold, by having states and seasons when I was clear that Biddy was immeasurably better

204
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:28,159
than Estella, and that the plain honest working life to which I was born had nothing in it to

205
00:18:28,159 --> 00:18:35,000
be ashamed of, but offered me sufficient means of self-respect and happiness. At those times,

206
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:40,638
I would decide conclusively that my disaffection to dear old Joe and the forge was gone, and that

207
00:18:40,638 --> 00:18:46,559
I was growing up in a fair way to be partners with Joe and to keep company with Biddy,—when

208
00:18:46,559 --> 00:18:52,719
all in a moment some confounding remembrance of the Havisham days would fall upon me like a

209
00:18:52,719 --> 00:19:01,798
destructive missile, and scatter my wits again. Scattered wits take a long time picking up; and

210
00:19:01,798 --> 00:19:07,559
often before I had got them well together, they would be dispersed in all directions by one stray

211
00:19:07,559 --> 00:19:13,520
thought, that perhaps after all Miss Havisham was going to make my fortune when my time was out.

212
00:19:13,520 --> 00:19:17,359
If my time had run out, it would have left me still at the height of my perplexities,

213
00:19:17,359 --> 00:19:22,637
I dare say. It never did run out, however, but was brought to a premature end,

214
00:19:22,638 --> 00:19:25,000
as I proceed to relate. Chapter 18.
